From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:00:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: apple-dart: let a host power-cycle its J713
 DART for a deep sleep

A deep sleep with a Thunderbolt dock attached powers off the ACIO and
tunneled PCIe DARTs and the external display coprocessors' DARTs, and
their hosts have to bring them back before the first requester runs
again. Runtime PM cannot do it: the host decides when its requesters
are stopped and when its clocks are back.

Add an owner interface for the T8110 DARTs whose device tree node names
a clock protection slice, other than the internal display ones. The
host binds once, then around a deep sleep calls prepare (before any
suspend callback), off (requesters stopped, clocks still on), on
(clocks back, before any DMA) and finish. off closes the DART to
hardware commands, saves the stream table, tunables and partition
words, disables the streams, flushes, and releases the clock-protection
slice. on restores the slice and the saved state and reopens the DART.
The generic suspend and resume callbacks leave an owned DART alone, and
a DART without a bound owner takes no extra lock on its map paths.

While a DART is closed, its page tables are still updated, in memory
only: map, unmap, attach, release and free never touch its registers.
Those writers are counted. Reopening, after a restore or after an off
that failed before changing anything, waits for them, and if anything
changed meanwhile flushes the whole TLB before the DART opens. finish
always ends the transaction. A DART that was not restored stays closed,
its registers unused, until reboot, even once its owner is gone;
nothing panics on it.

Things learned on the J713 that the restore accounts for:

- The tunneled PCIe DART has 256 stream slots where Linux allocates 64.
  The whole bank, as the hardware reports it, is saved, the reserved
  bypass streams included.
- An external display DART's clock-protection slice word reads zero
  after the sleep. It is saved at off and written back at on.
- A locked DART can come back with other bits set in the root of a
  stream that is not enabled and whose root was, and still is, invalid.
  That is accepted. Every control register and every valid root must
  match, and a locked DART is never rewritten.

The slice handling follows the internal display DART's power-down.
A failed restore dumps the registers that differ.

Add host-side models of the register sequence and of the ownership
rules, each with negative controls.

This builds on work by Bartosz Nitka.

